\citation{solt2024cascade}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Schedule coverage, audited run by run. Top: each cell is one run; filled is an interleaving not seen before in that row, hollow is a repeat of class $n$, and white hatching marks a run that hit the cycle budget without finishing the program. The deterministic row \emph  {is} the paper's thesis. PCT leads or ties on distinct schedules---but on \texttt  {mt-spinwait} and \texttt  {mt-crosscall} every one of its runs is hatched, so those counts are over truncated executions. Bottom: order-pair coverage, the coarser metric. PCT produces \emph  {fewer} distinct cross-hart orderings on \texttt  {mt-crosscall} (48 vs.\ 144): serialising harts explores different schedules without exploring different orderings.\relax }}{5}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:explore}{{4}{5}{Schedule coverage, audited run by run. Top: each cell is one run; filled is an interleaving not seen before in that row, hollow is a repeat of class $n$, and white hatching marks a run that hit the cycle budget without finishing the program. The deterministic row \emph {is} the paper's thesis. PCT leads or ties on distinct schedules---but on \texttt {mt-spinwait} and \texttt {mt-crosscall} every one of its runs is hatched, so those counts are over truncated executions. Bottom: order-pair coverage, the coarser metric. PCT produces \emph {fewer} distinct cross-hart orderings on \texttt {mt-crosscall} (48 vs.\ 144): serialising harts explores different schedules without exploring different orderings.\relax }{figure.caption.16}{}}
